            <div xml:id="d373">

                <pb facs="0778" n="748" xml:id="pg748"/>

                <head>373. Memorandum of a Conversation, Ambassador’s Residence, Paris, December 18,
                    1957, 10:30 a.m.<anchor n="1" xml:id="d373-f1"/></head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">USDel/MC/33</seg>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#italic"
                            >Paris</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            type="conversation-or-meeting-date" when="1957-12-18T10:30:00+01:00"><hi
                                rendition="#italic">December 18, 1957, 10:30
                        a.m.</hi></date></dateline>
                </opener>
                <list subtype="participants" type="description">
                    <label>PARTICIPANTS</label>
                    <item><hi rendition="#italic">United States</hi>
                        <list>
                            <item>The President</item>
                            <item>Mr. Rockwell</item>
                        </list></item>
                    <item><hi rendition="#italic">Turkey</hi>
                        <list>
                            <item>Mr. Adnan Menderes, Prime
                                Minister of Turkey</item>
                            <item>Mr. Fatin Zorlu, Foreign
                                Minister of Turkey</item>
                            <item>Mr. Melih Esenbel, Secretary
                                General of the Turkish Foreign Office</item>
                        </list></item>
                </list>
                <list subtype="subject" type="description">
                    <label>SUBJECT</label>
                    <item>Egypt</item>
                </list>
                <p>Prime Minister Menderes said that we must
                    not accept a fait accompli in Syria. The President thought that there might be
                    some possibility of hopeful developments there. He knew how staunchly Turkey had
                    opposed the Communist penetration of Syria. Mr. Menderes said that Turkey was gratified by the way the United
                    States had stood by Turkey. The USSR had not failed to notice this.</p>
                <p>Mr. Menderes commented that Nuri Said had just been in Turkey and had said
                    that his recent talks with the President and the Secretary in Washington<anchor
                        n="2" xml:id="d373-f2"/> about Syria had been most satisfactory.</p>
                <p>Mr. Menderes said that Turkey had no
                    illusion about NATO’s abilities to be helpful
                    in the Middle East. NATO was divided, but US
                    policy must continue strong in the Middle East.</p>
                <p>The President asked what Mr. Menderes
                    thought of the military situation in Syria. The Prime Minister said that the
                    Russians have sent Syria more arms than Syria can use. The Soviets have not made
                    Syria into a Communist satellite yet, because they are afraid of public reaction
                    in the Arab world. They have put their hand on Syria, however, and can come in
                    at any time.</p>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d373-f1" n="1" type="source" xml:id="d373-f1-content">
                <p>Source: Department of State, President’s Memoranda of Conversation: Lot 66 D 149,
                    1956–1959. Secret. Drafted by Rockwell.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d373-f2" n="2" xml:id="d373-f2-content">
                <p>On December 5 and 10, Nuri discussed the
                    Arab-Israeli conflict and related matters with Dulles in
                    Washington.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
